  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930198",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-72105",
      "knowledge_point_id": "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782",
      "knowledge_point": "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change",
      "problem": "Douglas, a pilot, is flying a plane. The plane's control systems record two measurements for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in minutes, since take off. The two measurements are:\n\n- $A(t)$ - the altitude (height) of the plane\n\n- $r(t)$ the rate at which the altitude is increasing\n\nWhat is the relationship between $A(t)$ and $r(t)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The function $A(t)$ gives the altitude of the plane after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}A(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }A}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${A}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the altitude is increasing at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $A(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${A}^{′}(t)=r(t).$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{A}^{′}(t)=-r(t)",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "A(t)={r}^{′}(t)",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "A(t)=r(t)",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "A(t)=-{r}^{′}(t)",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{A}^{′}(t)=r(t)",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "easy",
