      "math_academy_id": "q-170494",
      "knowledge_point_id": "54588722-bb80-54ca-b100-03aca39e0c17",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Fourth Quadrant",
      "problem": "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=9-6\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930486/assets/1bc5f38d44d9df32a7493f8d210bbc17d716d8a63316e3d730a1c4258d56fe46.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡(\\frac{6}{9})=arctan⁡(\\frac{2}{3})≈0.588\\,00.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.588\\,00≈5.70\n$$\n\nto two decimal places.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "4.99",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "5.70",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "5.35",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
