    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-35192",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4})=\\infty .\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
