      "math_academy_id": "q-102192",
      "knowledge_point_id": "852449ca-b4df-5142-9540-bb503d5b7b62",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Negative Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule",
      "problem": "If $y=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}){x}^{(-\\frac{3}{2}-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
